-- Q1: medium / L2 — Two-class method: allocating undistributed earnings to participating securities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Stern Capital Corp. has net income of $700,000, 200,000 weighted-average common shares, and 50,000 participating preferred shares entitled to share equally in undistributed earnings. Preferred dividends of $50,000 were declared. Under the two-class method, what amount of earnings is allocated to common shareholders for basic EPS?', '["$520,000", "$650,000", "$700,000", "$560,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-61A, participating securities require the two-class method. Undistributed earnings of $650,000 ($700,000 − $50,000) are allocated pro rata: common receives $650,000 × (200,000 / 250,000) = $520,000. Choice B ($650,000) is incorrect because it fails to allocate the undistributed earnings between common and participating preferred shares.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Convertible debt issued mid-year: partial-year if-converted adjustments
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pineview Industries issues $600,000 face value, 5% convertible bonds on July 1, Year 1. Each $1,000 bond converts into 20 common shares. The tax rate is 25%. For diluted EPS, what are the numerator adjustment and incremental shares for these bonds for Year 1?', '["$15,000 added to numerator and 12,000 incremental shares", "$11,250 added to numerator and 12,000 incremental shares", "$11,250 added to numerator and 6,000 incremental shares", "$15,000 added to numerator and 6,000 incremental shares"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method adjusts for the period the bonds are outstanding. Interest saved is $600,000 × 5% × 6/12 = $15,000, net of tax = $11,250. Incremental shares are 600 bonds × 20 shares × 6/12 = 6,000. Choice B ($11,250 and 12,000 shares) is incorrect because it fails to weight the shares for the partial year the bonds were outstanding.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Options exercised during the period: partial-period treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Brookhaven Corp. had 15,000 stock options outstanding on January 1 with an exercise price of $25. The average market price was $50. On July 1, all 15,000 options were exercised. For diluted EPS, how many incremental shares are added to the denominator for these options?', '["7,500", "3,750", "15,000", "5,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-26, options exercised during the period use the treasury stock method for the period they were outstanding as options. Incremental shares = 15,000 − (15,000 × $25 / $50) = 7,500, weighted for 6 months = 3,750. Choice A (7,500) is incorrect because it applies the treasury stock method correctly but fails to weight the incremental shares for the partial period before exercise.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Definition of a simple capital structure
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which statement best describes a simple capital structure?', '["A structure in which only common stock is authorized", "A structure with fewer than three classes of equity securities", "A structure in which no preferred stock dividends were declared", "A structure with no outstanding securities that could potentially dilute basic EPS"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, a simple capital structure has only common stock outstanding with no potentially dilutive securities such as options, warrants, or convertible instruments. Choice A is incorrect because a company may have preferred stock outstanding and still be simple if that preferred stock has no conversion feature or other dilutive potential.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Share repurchase and reissue mid-year: weighted-average calculation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Garfield Manufacturing had 500,000 shares outstanding on January 1. On March 1, the company repurchased 60,000 shares as treasury stock. On November 1, it reissued 30,000 treasury shares. What is the weighted-average number of shares for the year?', '["455,000", "470,000", "500,000", "440,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, treasury stock reduces shares outstanding from the repurchase date. Weighted-average = (500,000 × 2/12) + (440,000 × 8/12) + (470,000 × 2/12) = 83,333 + 293,333 + 78,333 = 455,000 (rounded). Choice B (470,000) is incorrect because it uses only the ending share balance without weighting for the periods each amount was outstanding.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: hard / L4 — Written put options: reverse treasury stock method evaluation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Lakewood Enterprises wrote put options during Year 1 obligating it to repurchase 20,000 shares at $45 per share. The average market price during Year 1 was $35 per share. Under ASC 260, evaluate the diluted EPS treatment of these written puts using the reverse treasury stock method.', '["The written puts are antidilutive because the exercise price exceeds the market price and should be excluded from diluted EPS", "The written puts are dilutive, adding approximately 5,714 incremental shares because the company must issue more shares at market price to fund the higher-priced repurchase", "The written puts add 20,000 shares to the diluted EPS denominator because all shares subject to repurchase must be included", "The written puts reduce the numerator by $200,000 but do not affect the denominator"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, written put options use the reverse treasury stock method when in-the-money. Assumed proceeds = 20,000 × $45 = $900,000; shares issued at market = $900,000 / $35 = 25,714; incremental shares = 25,714 − 20,000 = 5,714. Choice A is incorrect because written puts are dilutive (not antidilutive) when the exercise price exceeds market price, the opposite of call options.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q7: hard / L4 — Multiple dilutive securities: evaluate sequential inclusion order
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ridgewood Corp. reports net income of $1,000,000 with 250,000 common shares. It has: Security A (options adding 8,000 incremental shares, per-share impact $0), Security B ($200,000 face 6% convertible bonds converting to 15,000 shares, tax rate 25%, per-share impact $0.60), and Security C ($100,000 par convertible preferred with $8,000 annual dividend converting to 12,000 shares, per-share impact $0.67). Basic EPS is $4.00. Evaluate the proper inclusion order and diluted EPS.', '["Include all three in order A, B, C, producing diluted EPS of $3.57", "Include only A and B because Security C is antidilutive when tested after A and B", "Include all three in order A, C, B because convertible preferred has priority over convertible debt", "Include only Security A because both convertible securities are antidilutive"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-44, potentially dilutive securities are ranked by per-share impact (lowest first): A ($0), B ($0.60), C ($0.67). After including all three sequentially, each reduces EPS below the prior step: $3.88 → $3.70 → $3.57. All remain dilutive. Choice B is incorrect because Security C''s per-share impact of $0.67 is below diluted EPS at each inclusion step, so it remains dilutive.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Year-end stock dividend: retroactive restatement before issuance
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Crossfield Corp. had 200,000 shares outstanding throughout Year 1. On December 20, Year 1, it declared a 20% stock dividend, distributing 40,000 shares on January 10, Year 2 (before Year 1 financial statements were issued). Net income was $960,000 with no preferred stock. What is basic EPS for Year 1?', '["$4.80", "$4.36", "$4.00", "$3.69"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock dividends are applied retroactively to the entire period, even if distributed after year-end but before the financial statements are issued. Weighted-average shares = 200,000 × 1.20 = 240,000. EPS = $960,000 / 240,000 = $4.00. Choice A ($4.80) is incorrect because it uses the pre-dividend share count of 200,000, ignoring the required retroactive adjustment.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: hard / L4 — Loss from continuing operations controls dilution testing
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Grandview Holdings reports a net loss from continuing operations of $350,000 and income from discontinued operations of $500,000, producing net income of $150,000. The company has 100,000 common shares and 8,000 stock options (exercise price $20, average market price $40) producing 4,000 incremental shares under the treasury stock method. Evaluate the proper diluted EPS treatment.', '["Include the options because net income is positive, producing diluted EPS of $1.44", "Include the options only for the discontinued operations line item", "Exclude the options from continuing operations but include them for the discontinued operations EPS", "Exclude the options from all diluted EPS line items because the control number — loss from continuing operations — makes all potential shares antidilutive"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-19, the control number for determining dilution is income from continuing operations (after preferred dividends). When this amount is a loss, all potentially dilutive securities are antidilutive and excluded from diluted EPS for all line items. Choice A is incorrect because using net income as the control number violates ASC 260; the standard requires income from continuing operations.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — Actual bond conversion: effect on basic vs diluted EPS relationship
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Hawthorn Industries had $500,000 of 8% convertible bonds outstanding on January 1, convertible into 25,000 common shares. On April 1, all bonds were converted. Hawthorn had 200,000 common shares before conversion, net income of $900,000, and a 25% tax rate. How does the actual conversion affect the relationship between basic and diluted EPS?', '["Diluted EPS is higher than basic EPS because interest expense savings increase net income", "Diluted EPS is lower than basic EPS because the if-converted method assumes conversion from the beginning of the year, adding pre-conversion interest net of tax and pre-conversion shares", "Basic and diluted EPS are identical because the bonds were actually converted during the period", "Diluted EPS cannot be computed because the bonds are no longer outstanding at year-end"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, when bonds convert during the period, diluted EPS assumes conversion from the beginning of the year. This adds back 3 months of after-tax interest ($7,500) and 3 months of shares (6,250), reducing diluted EPS below basic. Choice C is incorrect because the if-converted method still applies for the pre-conversion period, creating a difference between basic and diluted EPS.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: medium / L3 — Assumed proceeds include unrecognized compensation cost
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Willowbrook Inc. has 20,000 employee stock options with an exercise price of $30 and an average market price of $60. Unamortized compensation cost related to these options is $80,000. Under ASC 260-10-45-29, analyze the effect of including unamortized compensation on the incremental share count.', '["Unrecognized compensation increases incremental shares because it reduces assumed proceeds", "Unrecognized compensation has no effect because only cash proceeds from exercise are relevant", "Unrecognized compensation decreases incremental shares from 10,000 to approximately 8,667 because it increases assumed proceeds, allowing more treasury shares to be repurchased", "Unrecognized compensation decreases incremental shares from 10,000 to 6,000 because the cost is subtracted from shares issued"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-29, assumed proceeds for the treasury stock method include exercise price plus unamortized compensation. Total proceeds = $600,000 + $80,000 = $680,000. Shares repurchased = $680,000 / $60 = 11,333. Incremental = 20,000 − 11,333 = 8,667. Choice A is incorrect because unamortized compensation increases (not decreases) assumed proceeds, resulting in fewer incremental shares.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Warrants: treasury stock method calculation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Magnolia Corp. has 150,000 weighted-average common shares, net income of $450,000, no preferred dividends, and 12,000 warrants outstanding with an exercise price of $35. The average market price is $70. Using the treasury stock method, what is diluted EPS?', '["$2.78", "$3.00", "$2.93", "$2.88"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, warrants use the treasury stock method identically to stock options. Proceeds = 12,000 × $35 = $420,000; shares repurchased = $420,000 / $70 = 6,000; incremental = 6,000. Diluted EPS = $450,000 / 156,000 = $2.88. Choice A ($2.78) is incorrect because it adds all 12,000 warrant shares without subtracting the shares assumed repurchased with exercise proceeds.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Contingently issuable shares: conditions met at interim but not year-end
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Redwood Partners agreed to issue 25,000 additional shares to selling shareholders if cumulative revenue exceeds $5,000,000 by December 31, Year 2. At June 30, Year 2, cumulative revenue was $5,200,000, but a large Q4 return reduced year-end cumulative revenue to $4,800,000. How should these shares be treated in the annual EPS calculation?', '["Excluded from both basic and diluted EPS because the revenue condition was not met at December 31", "Included in basic EPS for the full year because the revenue condition was met at an interim date", "Included in basic EPS for the first half of the year only (25,000 x 6/12 = 12,500 shares)", "Included in diluted EPS for the full year because the conditions were satisfied at some point during the period"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48 and 45-52, contingently issuable shares are included in basic EPS only if all conditions are met at the reporting period end. Since year-end revenue of $4,800,000 is below the $5,000,000 target, the shares are excluded. Choice D is incorrect because meeting the condition at an interim date does not satisfy the requirement that conditions be met at the end of the reporting period.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — Retroactive EPS adjustment for stock split after year-end
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ashland Corp. reported basic EPS of $6.00 in its Year 1 income statement. In February Year 2, before the Year 1 financial statements were issued, Ashland announced a 2-for-1 stock split. Analyze how this event affects the EPS reported in the Year 1 financial statements.', '["Year 1 EPS remains $6.00 because the split is a Year 2 event", "Year 1 EPS remains $6.00 with disclosure of the split in the notes only", "Year 1 EPS is retroactively adjusted to $3.00 only when presented comparatively in the Year 2 statements", "Year 1 EPS is retroactively adjusted to $3.00 in the Year 1 financial statements"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock splits occurring after the balance sheet date but before financial statements are issued must be retroactively reflected in the EPS calculation. Year 1 EPS is restated from $6.00 to $3.00. Choice C is incorrect because the adjustment applies to the Year 1 statements themselves, not only when presented comparatively in future periods.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Which entities must present EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which entities are required to present earnings per share on the face of the income statement?', '["All entities that issue financial statements", "All entities with more than $10 million in total revenue", "Entities with publicly traded common stock or potential common stock", "Only entities registered with the SEC"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, EPS presentation is required only for entities with publicly traded common stock or potential common stock (such as options or convertible securities). Private companies are exempt. Choice D is incorrect because SEC registration alone is not the criterion; the standard applies to any entity with publicly traded common equity or potential common equity.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: easy / L1 — Definition of weighted-average common shares outstanding
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'What does the weighted-average number of common shares outstanding measure for EPS purposes?', '["The total number of shares authorized by the corporate charter", "The shares outstanding adjusted for the portion of the period each share count was outstanding", "The number of shares outstanding at the end of the reporting period", "The average of beginning and ending shares outstanding"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, the weighted-average reflects shares outstanding for the fraction of the period they were outstanding, accounting for issuances and repurchases at their respective dates. Choice D is incorrect because simply averaging beginning and ending balances ignores the timing of share transactions during the period.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — What is subtracted from net income for basic EPS numerator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which item is subtracted from net income when computing the basic EPS numerator?', '["Preferred stock dividends", "Common stock dividends declared", "Treasury stock purchases during the period", "Interest on convertible bonds"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, the basic EPS numerator is net income minus preferred stock dividends (whether declared or, for cumulative preferred, accumulated). Choice B is incorrect because common dividends reduce retained earnings but are not subtracted from the EPS numerator; they represent a distribution of earnings already captured in net income.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — Definition of antidilutive securities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, an antidilutive security is one whose inclusion in the diluted EPS calculation would:', '["Decrease the EPS numerator below zero", "Require a restatement of prior-period EPS", "Reduce the weighted-average share count", "Increase diluted EPS above basic EPS"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-17, antidilutive securities are those whose assumed conversion or exercise would increase EPS (or decrease a loss per share). Such securities must be excluded from diluted EPS. Choice A is incorrect because whether the numerator becomes negative is not the antidilution test; the test compares diluted EPS to basic EPS.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: hard / L4 — Mandatorily redeemable preferred stock: evaluate EPS classification impact
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Evergreen Holdings issued 50,000 shares of mandatorily redeemable preferred stock at $40 per share. Under ASC 480, these shares are classified as liabilities, and the $200,000 annual dividend is reported as interest expense. The company has net income of $1,800,000 (after the mandatorily redeemable dividend), 400,000 common shares, and $100,000 in regular preferred dividends. Evaluate the proper EPS numerator treatment.', '["Subtract both the $200,000 and $100,000 preferred dividends, producing EPS of $3.75", "Subtract only the $200,000 mandatorily redeemable dividends, producing EPS of $4.00", "Subtract only the $100,000 regular preferred dividends because the mandatorily redeemable dividends are already reflected as interest expense in net income, producing EPS of $4.25", "Make no preferred dividend adjustments because all dividends are already in net income, producing EPS of $4.50"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 480-10-25 and ASC 260-10-45-11, mandatorily redeemable preferred classified as a liability has its dividends treated as interest expense, already deducted in arriving at net income. Only the $100,000 regular preferred dividends are subtracted. EPS = ($1,800,000 − $100,000) / 400,000 = $4.25. Choice A is incorrect because it double-counts the mandatorily redeemable dividends that are already reflected in net income.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Discontinued operations: EPS presentation requirements
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pinecrest Industries reports income from continuing operations of $720,000, a loss from discontinued operations (net of tax) of $180,000, and net income of $540,000 with 120,000 weighted-average common shares and no preferred stock. Which analysis of EPS presentation requirements is correct?', '["Only net income EPS of $4.50 is reported on the face of the income statement", "EPS for continuing operations ($6.00) and net income ($4.50) are reported on the face, with discontinued operations EPS in the notes", "EPS must be reported for all three line items on the face of the income statement", "EPS for continuing operations ($6.00) and net income ($4.50) must be on the face; discontinued operations EPS ($1.50 loss) may be on the face or in the notes"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2 and 45-3, entities must present EPS for income from continuing operations and net income on the face of the income statement. EPS for discontinued operations may be presented on the face or disclosed in the notes. Choice C is incorrect because ASC 260 does not require all three EPS figures on the face of the income statement.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: easy / L2 — Basic EPS with stock dividend: simple retroactive application
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Oakmont Corp. had 100,000 shares outstanding for the entire year, declared a 10% stock dividend on June 15, and reported net income of $550,000 with no preferred dividends. What is basic EPS?', '["$5.00", "$5.50", "$5.24", "$4.58"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock dividends are applied retroactively to the entire period. Weighted-average shares = 100,000 × 1.10 = 110,000. EPS = $550,000 / 110,000 = $5.00. Choice B ($5.50) is incorrect because it uses the pre-dividend share count of 100,000, ignoring the retroactive adjustment required for stock dividends.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — Convertible preferred becomes antidilutive when income drops
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Dalton Manufacturing has net income of $110,000 with 150,000 common shares and 5,000 shares of $100 par, 7% convertible preferred stock (each converting into 10 common shares). Basic EPS is $0.50 after deducting $35,000 in preferred dividends. In Year 1, when net income was $500,000, including this preferred stock was dilutive. Analyze whether it remains dilutive in the current year.', '["It is dilutive because the conversion terms are unchanged from the prior year", "It is antidilutive because the per-share impact of conversion ($0.70) exceeds basic EPS ($0.50), so inclusion would increase diluted EPS above basic EPS", "It is dilutive because converting preferred always reduces EPS by eliminating the dividend deduction", "It is antidilutive only if the market price of the preferred stock exceeds its par value"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-17, a security is antidilutive if its inclusion increases EPS. The per-share impact is $35,000 / 50,000 = $0.70, which exceeds basic EPS of $0.50. Including it would raise diluted EPS to $0.55 ($110,000 / 200,000), so it is excluded. Choice A is incorrect because dilutiveness depends on the relationship between per-share impact and current-period basic EPS, not on prior-year results.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — What the treasury stock method assumes about option proceeds
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under the treasury stock method used for diluted EPS, what is assumed about the proceeds received from the exercise of stock options?', '["The proceeds are used to retire outstanding long-term debt", "The proceeds are distributed as a special dividend to shareholders", "The proceeds are invested in marketable securities", "The proceeds are used to repurchase common shares at the average market price"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method assumes that proceeds from the hypothetical exercise of options are used to repurchase common shares at the average market price during the period. Choice A is incorrect because the standard specifically prescribes share repurchase, not debt retirement, as the assumed use of proceeds.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: medium / L2 — If-converted method with convertible preferred: add back dividends
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Sycamore Holdings has net income of $840,000, 200,000 common shares, and 10,000 shares of $50 par, 8% convertible preferred stock, each converting into 3 common shares. Preferred dividends of $40,000 were declared. Using the if-converted method, what is diluted EPS?', '["$4.00", "$3.48", "$3.65", "$3.82"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method for convertible preferred adds back dividends to the numerator and adds converted shares to the denominator. Numerator = $840,000 (net income, no dividend deduction); denominator = 200,000 + 30,000 = 230,000. Diluted EPS = $840,000 / 230,000 = $3.65. Choice B ($3.48) is incorrect because it uses $800,000 as the numerator, failing to add back the $40,000 preferred dividends assumed eliminated by conversion.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q25: hard / L3 — Contracts settleable in shares or cash: dilutive effect analysis
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Mercer Group has a forward contract obligating it to repurchase 15,000 of its own shares at $80 per share, settleable in either shares or cash at Mercer''s option. The average market price is $60 per share. Under ASC 260-10-45-35, analyze the most likely effect on diluted EPS.', '["The contract adds approximately 5,000 incremental dilutive shares because the repurchase price exceeds the market price, requiring more shares to be issued at market to fund the repurchase", "The contract is antidilutive and excluded because the repurchase price exceeds the market price, similar to out-of-the-money options", "The contract has no effect on diluted EPS because management can choose cash settlement to avoid dilution", "The contract reduces the denominator by 15,000 shares because the entity is committed to repurchasing them"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, share-settleable repurchase contracts in-the-money are assumed share-settled and use the reverse treasury stock method. Shares issued = ($80 × 15,000) / $60 = 20,000; net incremental = 20,000 − 15,000 = 5,000. Choice C is incorrect because ASC 260 presumes the more dilutive settlement method regardless of management''s stated intent.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Required income statement line items for EPS presentation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, for which line items must earnings per share be presented on the face of the income statement?', '["Net income only", "Gross profit and net income", "Operating income and net income", "Income from continuing operations and net income"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, entities must present basic and diluted EPS on the face of the income statement for income from continuing operations and for net income. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 requires EPS for both income from continuing operations and net income, not just net income alone.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Actual bond conversion mid-year: weighted-average shares for basic EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Heritage Corp. had 300,000 common shares outstanding on January 1. On August 1, holders of $400,000 face value convertible bonds (each $1,000 bond converts into 30 shares) converted all bonds into common stock. What is the weighted-average number of common shares for basic EPS?', '["312,000", "305,000", "300,000", "306,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares from actual conversion are included from the conversion date. Conversion shares = 400 bonds × 30 = 12,000. Weighted-average = (300,000 × 7/12) + (312,000 × 5/12) = 175,000 + 130,000 = 305,000. Choice D (306,000) is incorrect because it weights the conversion from July 1 rather than the actual August 1 conversion date.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: hard / L4 — Evaluate whether participating securities require the two-class method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Fernwood Corp. has 400,000 common shares and 80,000 Series A preferred shares. The Series A receives a $2.00 annual dividend and participates share-for-share in common dividends above $1.00 per share. Net income is $1,600,000. Common dividends of $1.50 per share and preferred dividends of $200,000 (including $40,000 participation) were declared. Evaluate whether the two-class method is required and its impact versus the traditional deduction approach.', '["The two-class method is not required because the preferred receives a fixed dividend, so the traditional method applies, producing EPS of $3.50", "The two-class method is required but produces the same result as the traditional method when all earnings are distributed", "The two-class method is required because the Series A participates in undistributed earnings, reducing basic EPS from $3.50 under the traditional method to approximately $3.17", "The two-class method is optional and applies only when the participation dividend exceeds 10% of the base preferred dividend"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-61A, the two-class method is required for participating securities. Undistributed earnings = $1,600,000 − $800,000 = $800,000, allocated to common: $800,000 × (400,000 / 480,000) = $666,667. Total common = $600,000 + $666,667 = $1,266,667; EPS = $3.17. Choice A is incorrect because the participation feature makes the two-class method mandatory, and the traditional deduction method overstates common shareholder earnings.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q29: medium / L3 — Rights issue with below-market price: bonus element analysis
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Thornburg Corp. had 500,000 shares outstanding and issued rights to existing shareholders offering one new share for every five held at $30 per share. The market price was $50 before the rights offering. Analyze how the below-market element in this rights issue affects the EPS calculation.', '["The below-market element creates a bonus factor requiring retroactive adjustment of prior-period EPS, similar to a stock dividend, because the price decline from $50 to $46.67 represents embedded free shares", "No adjustment is needed because rights issues are new share issuances at the exercise date with no retroactive restatement", "The full 100,000 new shares are treated as a stock dividend retroactively applied to all prior periods", "Only the difference between the rights price and par value determines the bonus element"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-17, a rights issue at below-market price contains a bonus element analogous to a stock dividend. The theoretical ex-rights price = (5 × $50 + 1 × $30) / 6 = $46.67. The adjustment factor ($50 / $46.67 = 1.0714) is applied retroactively to prior-period EPS. Choice B is incorrect because below-market rights include a gratuitous element that requires retroactive restatement, unlike a pure market-price issuance.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q30: easy / L1 — Cumulative preferred dividends always reduce basic EPS numerator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which type of preferred stock dividend must be deducted from net income when computing basic EPS, regardless of whether dividends are declared?', '["Noncumulative preferred dividends", "Cumulative preferred dividends", "Participating preferred dividends", "Convertible preferred dividends"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, cumulative preferred dividends are deducted from net income for basic EPS whether or not declared, because the cumulative right to dividends accrues regardless of declaration. Choice A is incorrect because noncumulative preferred dividends are deducted only when actually declared by the board of directors.', 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;
